what_i_did:
1. Analysed the complete cal-1 data (read-only, old machine). This is 8,800 cells: 11 arms × 200 SearchQA training items × 4 runs, from attempt-1 (7,189 with verdicts) and attempt-2 (1,611). I computed:
   - per-arm flip rates and text- and answer-level identity;
   - the parent:A − parent:B null: histogram, all 1-, 2- and 4-run realizations, and a dispersion permutation test against 20,000 re-splits;
   - the analytic and resampled SD of the 200-item paired mean at 1/2/3/4/8/16 runs;
   - exact unbiased majority-of-3 moments (U-statistics over disjoint triples) and majority vs mean t on 9 real comparisons;
   - run exchangeability, session, time-gap, first-call, retry and latency checks;
   - variance split and item heterogeneity σ²_het per arm;
   - noise on failure-enriched panels;
   - the per-token divergence hazard.
2. Measured per-benchmark noise from the completed v3 run, training-side units only (train/screen/confirm). An assertion guards against any evaluation/ or shared/ path.
3. Discovered and quantified serving-mode drift. Grouped all scaffold and task-card calls by session, with the request bytes identity-verified, and ran paired same-item comparisons across sessions plus a within-cal-1 autocorrelation test.
4. Ran my own route pilot from the new machine: 266 calls, 319,043 tokens, training items only. Every request was SHA-256-verified against cal-1 before sending.
   - P0: 10 parameter-acceptance probes.
   - P1: 160 calls, 8 items × 4 repeats × {T0, T1, T1+seed, T1+top_k=1, T1+top_p=1e-6}.
   - P2: 96 calls, 12 mixed items × 4 rounds × {12-worker burst, sequential}.
   - I also analysed the earlier D5 attempt's 288-call pilot, plus fingerprint and prefix-cache analyses.
5. Verified vLLM as the likely backend from the verbatim validator messages in vLLM source. Read Thinking Machines, the vLLM batch-invariance docs, the SGLang deterministic-inference blog, the DeepSeek API docs, and Miller, Atil and Yuan.
6. Built the item-count tables over effect size × noise × runs × heterogeneity, the per-benchmark MDE with the whole training pool, and per-call costs.
7. Wrote the report. I modified no repository file, wrote nothing under /data/coevo5-*, and read no test or holdout pool.
